{{nihongo|'''Kagetora Ishimoto'''|石本 影虎|''Ishimoto Kagetora''|11 September 1892 – 17 June 1975}} was a Sanese politician and bureaucrat who served as the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|40th Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from November 1958 until his resignation in October 1960. A member of the [[Okaiken Democratic Party]], he represented the Seto 3rd district in the [[House of Commoners (Okaiken)|House of Commoners]]. Ishimoto was the younger brother of [[Tokuma Ishimoto]], who served as Prime Minister from 1937 to 1939.

Ishimoto's tenure as Prime Minister was characterized by the early stages of Okaiken's post-war economic redevelopment. His government continued policies aimed at suppressing communist influence and discouraging organized labor, while focusing on rebuilding key industries damaged during the [[Great Adonian War]]. Ishimoto is notably remembered for championing the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] project, providing political and financial backing through the Second Shinkansen Act of 1957 despite significant opposition that derided the initiative as a "national folly." He resigned due to health reasons in October 1960, and was succeeded by [[Tadayama Ishida]] as a caretaker Prime Minister.

== Early life and education ==
Kagetora Ishimoto was born in 1892 in [[Osaki, Seto|Osaki]], a city in [[Seto Prefecture]], to a politically prominent family. His older brother, [[Tokuma Ishimoto]], would later serve as the 16th Prime Minister of Okaiken from 1937 to 1939. The Ishimoto family had deep roots in the political establishment of the southern Yagikoshu region.

Ishimoto pursued higher education at [[Okami University]] in the capital, where he studied political science and public administration. During his university years, he developed connections with future political leaders and members of the influential Democratic Party establishment.

== Early political career ==
Following his graduation, Ishimoto entered the civil service as a bureaucrat in the Ministry of Commerce and Industry. His administrative career was interrupted by the [[Great Adonian War]], during which he served in a civilian capacity coordinating industrial production for the war effort.

After the war ended in 1952, Ishimoto transitioned to electoral politics, winning a seat in the House of Commoners representing the Seto 3rd district. He aligned himself with the [[Okaiken Democratic Party]] (DPO), the same party his brother had led during his brief premiership two decades earlier. Despite the Ishimoto family's diminished political standing following Tokuma's death in 1939, Kagetora gradually rebuilt their influence within the DPO ranks.

Throughout the 1950s, Ishimoto gained a reputation as a pragmatic technocrat focused on economic development issues. He served in several junior ministerial positions before being tapped for higher office following the resignation of Prime Minister [[Ichirō Hatoyama]] in late 1958.

== Premiership (1958–1960) ==
{{Main|Ishimoto Cabinet}}

=== Appointment ===
On November 18, 1958, Ishimoto was appointed Prime Minister following internal party elections within the DPO. His selection represented a compromise between competing factions within the party, with his brother's legacy providing symbolic continuity while his technocratic credentials appealed to modernizers seeking to accelerate Okaiken's post-war recovery.

=== Economic policies ===
Ishimoto's government oversaw the early stages of Okaiken's post-war economic redevelopment, continuing policies initiated under his predecessor Hatoyama. His administration focused on rebuilding key industries damaged during the Great Adonian War, particularly steel, shipbuilding, and heavy machinery manufacturing.

A central element of Ishimoto's economic policy was the continuation of [[Illypnian influence on Okaiken|Western-influenced]] labor suppression policies. Drawing from precedents established in the immediate post-war period, Ishimoto's government actively discouraged organized labor movements and implemented measures to contain communist influence within trade unions. These policies, while controversial, were credited with maintaining industrial stability during a critical period of reconstruction.

=== The Harujima Shinkansen ===
{{Main|Harujima Shinkansen}}
Ishimoto's most significant legislative achievement was his backing of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] project. By the late 1950s, the post-war economic recovery had pushed the conventional narrow-gauge rail network to its breaking point, with the main Harujima corridor operating at 130% capacity. Engineers within Okaiken National Railways (ONR) proposed a radical solution: a completely separate standard-gauge line allowing unprecedented speeds.

The plan met fierce opposition, with many in government deriding it as a "national folly." However, Ishimoto provided crucial political and financial backing through the '''Second Shinkansen Act of 1957''' (enacted in 1958), overruling concerns about the project's enormous cost during a period of economic uncertainty. Construction began on October 11, 1957, under the auspices of the Ishimoto government, though Ishimoto himself would not live to see the line's completion in 1966, as it experienced significant delays during the [[Quiet Revolution]].

=== Health issues and resignation ===
Throughout 1960, Ishimoto's health began to deteriorate as he struggled with undiagnosed cardiovascular issues. The demands of the premiership, combined with long-standing conditions exacerbated by the war years, took an increasing toll on his stamina and ability to govern effectively. On October 12, 1960, Ishimoto formally submitted his resignation to Emperor Wakayama, citing health reasons.

He was succeeded by [[Tadayama Ishida]], who served as a caretaker Prime Minister tasked with managing internal party factions and preparing for the 1961 House of Commoners election. That election saw the Democratic Party lose power to the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]], ending the DPO's period of governance.

== Later life ==
After his resignation, Ishimoto retired from active politics but remained an influential elder statesman within Democratic Party circles. He occasionally provided counsel to subsequent DPO leadership and served on corporate boards during the economic boom years of the 1960s and early 1970s.

Ishimoto died in 1975 at his residence in Okami. He was survived by his wife and children. His funeral was attended by numerous political figures, including several former Prime Ministers and current cabinet members.

== Legacy ==
Historians generally regard Ishimoto's premiership as a transitional period in Okaiken's post-war history. While his term was brief and ended prematurely due to health concerns, his decisive support for the Shinkansen project is now viewed as a visionary investment that accelerated Okaiken's economic integration and modernization. The Harujima Shinkansen, though completed years after his death under very different political circumstances, stands as his most enduring policy legacy.

Critics note that his government's labor suppression policies, while successful in maintaining industrial stability, also entrenched patterns of worker subordination that would be challenged during the social upheavals of the late 1960s. The Ishimoto family's political influence waned significantly after his death, marking the end of a prominent dynastic presence in Okaiken politics that had begun with his brother's turn-of-the-century career.

